===Etymology===
Borrowed in the Middle Ages from a {{der|en|gml|-}} variant of [[Theodoric]], and revived in the nineteenth century.<ref>{{cite-book|en|2001|w:Patrick Hanks|author2=Flavia Hodges|A Concise Dictionary of First Names|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|edition=3rd|isbn=9780198662594}}</ref> Not related to {{nc|he|דֶּרֶךְ|tr=déreḵ||road}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛɹɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛɹɪk|s=2}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|male|from=Germanic languages}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|1895|||The American Magazine|publisher=w:Crowell-Collier Publishing Company||446|"Do you think so? Her husband has an odd name - '''Derek''' Keppel. He is a musician - a violinist."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1974|w:Joseph Heller|Something happened|isbn=0224010654||509|We do not entertain as much anymore because of '''Derek'''. (He produces strain. We have to pretend he doesn't.) I used to like him when I still thought he was normal. I was fond of him. I used to call him Dirk, and Kiddo, Steamshovel, Dinky Boy, and Dicky Dare. Till I found out what he was. Now it's always formal: '''Derek'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|2010|w:[[James Robertson (novelist)|James Robertson]]|And the Land Lay Still|publisher=Hamish Hamilton||isbn=9780241143568|292|Frederick or '''Derek''' Boothby was in his late fifties, the son of a naval officer and a daughter of the Earl of Limerick.}}
# {{surname|en}}.

